PATCH 625: preserve the direct digital channel setter's DS-relative byte lookup (board:0003).

Original 22ab assigns sample pointer/length, default or supplied pitch and cursor
before storing the byte SHR/ADD operands for the selected channel. Instructions
233c, 2354 and 2367 read BYTE DS:(1e8b+zero_extend(DL)), not a host address 1e8b.
Resolve this module operand through the existing shared fist_ext_addr owner.
Patch 623 already establishes byte-only writes; do not change their contract.

The first actual scripted channel-two assignment at tick 553 supplies sample
679d6, ECX=0, DL=3 and BL=2. Same-process paging-aware snapshots and a complete
25-instruction trace before RET 2376 prove nine module-byte changes, four saved
ESI stack bytes and no intervening interrupt. The complete sample matches the
provisioned DSOUNDS.BIN by offset/hash. The probe retains all original 600-ms
frames, final PCM and endpoint. The regression compares all isolated one-MiB
module bytes, full normalization instructions, the returned sample pointer and
the untouched 2048-byte physical DMA ring on native and WASM. Parent native
segfaults at the unbased lookup; WASM stores the wrong ADD operand.

This corrects the direct setter only. Queued/automatic channel allocation,
register transport, device initialization, IRQ/IF, elapsed instruction work and
final shared PCM remain open; no measured delay or missing producer is added.
